WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.200
Gaussian distribution.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.630
From sensor noise to neural network weights, one shape keeps showing up.

3
00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.433
Let's see why.

4
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:10.814
A Gaussian is the famous bell curve.

5
00:00:10.814 --> 00:00:12.933
It's described by just two numbers.

6
00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:15.834
Mu is the mean, where the bell is centered.

7
00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:19.100
Sigma is the standard deviation, how spread out it is.

8
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.467
The standard normal is the simplest case: mean zero, spread one.

9
00:00:24.667 --> 00:00:26.533
Here are two bells with the same mean.

10
00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:28.567
Mu decides where the bell sits.

11
00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:30.911
Sigma decides how wide it is.

12
00:00:30.911 --> 00:00:33.166
Bigger sigma, flatter and wider.

13
00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:35.633
That's the entire parameterization.

14
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:38.548
So why is the bell everywhere?

15
00:00:38.548 --> 00:00:40.033
The Central Limit Theorem.

16
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:44.533
Take many small, independent effects, whatever their individual shapes.

17
00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:46.500
Add them up, or average them.

18
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.778
The result tends toward a Gaussian.

19
00:00:48.778 --> 00:00:52.000
That's why averaging many measurements is so trustworthy.

20
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.567
Let's prove it in Python.

21
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:59.867
We average one thousand uniform noise readings, and repeat that ten thousand times.

22
00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:03.932
The averages center on zero point five, with a tiny spread.

23
00:01:03.932 --> 00:01:07.633
Plot them and a bell curve appears, from flat ingredients.

24
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:11.167
You'll meet Gaussians in three big places.

25
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:15.600
Sensor noise, because each reading blends many small physical effects.

26
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.200
Aggregated returns, which are sums of many small moves.

27
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.200
And weight initialization, where deep learning starts from a scaled Gaussian by default.

28
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.100
Here's how it plays out in a telemetry pipeline.

29
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.467
Each satellite temperature reading blends thousands of tiny effects, so its noise is nearly Gaussian.

30
00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:40.500
So the anomaly detector models each channel as a Gaussian and flags anything beyond three sigma.

31
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:48.800
The same distribution helps training: initial weights are drawn from a scaled Gaussian, so activations neither vanish nor explode.

32
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:50.788
Let's recap.

33
00:01:50.788 --> 00:01:54.533
A Gaussian is defined by its mean and standard deviation.

34
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:58.800
The Central Limit Theorem says sums of many small effects become a bell.

35
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.078
That's why noise models and weight initialization default to it.

36
00:02:03.078 --> 00:02:05.067
Next up: Bernoulli distribution.
